(Photo courtesy of Anadolu) KIGALI, Rwanda – Confirmed Ebola cases in the Democratic Republic of Congo have surpassed 12,000 since the outbreak was declared in May, as health authorities struggle to contain the country’s largest recorded outbreak amid escalating security challenges. The latest figures released by the Health Ministry on Thursday showed that cumulative infections&hellip;
Armed conflict and community resistance have hampered containment efforts, contributing to the spread of the virus from a single province in May to eight provinces: Haut-Uele, Ituri, North Kivu, South Kivu, Bas-Uele, Tshopo, South-Ubangi, and North Ubangi.
The World Health Organization (WHO) reported earlier this week that heavy fighting near a hospital in Ituri province had disrupted Ebola response operations, forcing its medical team to temporarily relocate from the Mongbwalu area until security conditions improve.
